# Shell scripts must keep LF endings.
#
# Git for Windows checks files out with CRLF by default. A shell script with
# CRLF fails on Linux in a way that reads as nonsense — the shebang becomes
# `/usr/bin/env bash\r`, which does not exist, and the error is
# "no such file or directory" naming an interpreter that plainly is there.
#
# scripts/tenant.sh provisions customer stacks on a Linux host from a checkout
# that may well have been made on Windows, so this is not hypothetical.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by Linux containers and tools that do not
# tolerate stray carriage returns.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
Caddyfile text eol=lf
*.caddy text eol=lf
docker-entrypoint.sh text eol=lf
